    <p style="margin:0 0 18px; color:#333333; font-size:15px; line-height:1.6;">Dear colleague,</p>

    <p style="margin:0 0 18px; color:#333333; font-size:15px; line-height:1.6;">We are pleased to invite you to our next webinar: <strong>Speed in Election Results Transmission: Getting Results Right, and Fast</strong>, taking place on <strong>Tuesday, 14 July 2026 at 13:00&ndash;15:00 UTC (14:00&ndash;16:00 BST)</strong>.</p>

    <p style="margin:0 0 18px; color:#333333; font-size:15px; line-height:1.6;">In the hours after polls close, an electoral management body (EMB) faces one of its hardest tests. The public, the media, and candidates all want results quickly &mdash; but every step from the polling station to the final declaration must be accurate, verifiable, and trusted. The gap between the close of polls and a credible result is where confidence is won or lost.</p>

    <p style="margin:0 0 18px; color:#333333; font-size:15px; line-height:1.6;">Technology has transformed what is possible. Electronic transmission, transparency servers, and real-time public dashboards can deliver results in hours rather than days. Yet speed introduces its own risks: a single error, an unexplained delay, or a poorly communicated figure can do lasting damage to public trust, however sound the underlying count.</p>

    <p style="margin:0 0 24px; color:#333333; font-size:15px; line-height:1.6;">ICPS brings together electoral practitioners and technology specialists to ask: how do EMBs deliver results quickly without sacrificing accuracy or trust &mdash; and what does good practice look like in 2026?</p>

